"Summer is matched to fire and the heart in the five-element framework, and its classic advice continues the arc this course's seasonal unit has followed from spring's growth to winter's storage: summer's job is full, outward growth, the peak of the yang half of the year. The Yellow Emperor's Inner Classic recommends rising early and going to bed a little later, similar to spring, but adds something spring didn't need: a caution against holding in frustration or resentment, described as letting qi \"vent\" freely the way summer's heat vents outward, rather than bottling it up during the season most associated with open, outward expression.": "b13aa00247d2",
"Summer is matched to fire and the heart in the five-element framework, and its classic advice continues the arc this course's seasonal unit has followed from spring's growth to winter's storage: summer's job is full, outward growth, the peak of the yang half of the year. The Yellow Emperor's Inner Classic recommends rising early and going to bed a little later, similar to spring, but adds something spring didn't need: a caution against holding in frustration or resentment, described as letting qi \"vent\" freely the way summer's heat vents outward, rather than bottling it up during the season most associated with open, outward expression. The physical caution for summer runs alongside the emotional one: because heat and sweating both drain qi and fluids directly, a concept covered in an earlier unit, summer creates a specific risk of losing too much of both at once. A person who sweats heavily through a hot day without adequately replacing fluids can end up with a combined qi-and-fluid deficiency by evening — tired, thirsty, and drained in a way that a cool room and a glass of water only partly fixes. This is why summer's advice isn't simply \"stay active and let things out,\" despite that being the season's basic direction. It's closer to \"let things out, but track what's leaving with them\" — venting frustration is encouraged, but venting fluids and qi without replacing them is not, and the two cautions sit side by side precisely because summer is the one season where a person can overdo the very thing the season is supposed to encourage.": "f76d9802aeae",
